            <ArticleTitle>Hypertensive crisis resulting from an MAO inhibitor and an over-the-counter appetite suppressant.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>482-4U</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We present the case of a 28-year-old man being treated with Nardil for chronic depression who developed a hypertensive crisis and a severe occipital headache one hour after ingesting an over-the-counter appetite suppressant. The adverse reactions between MAO inhibitors and phenylpropanolamine and discussed, as are the dangers of using Demerol to treat the headache and Aldomet to treat the hypertension.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
